<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-4-7-machine-learning">
  <time minutes="90" />
  <title>Machine Learning</title>

  <idx>machine-learning</idx>
  <introduction>
    <p>Quick Links:
      <xref text="global" ref="ml-outputs-from-data-and-probability,
      training-data-accuracy-privacy-bias, LOs-4-7, vocab-4-7"/>
    </p>
    <p>
      In this topic, we examine how data and probabilistic algorithms shape machine learning outputs,
      and how training-data choices affect accuracy, privacy, and bias.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="ml-outputs-from-data-and-probability">
    <title>How Data and Probabilistic Algorithms Shape Outputs</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.A.1</title>
      <p>
        Training data for machine learning can come from sources such as sensors, surveys,
        simulations, or existing data sets. Depending on the task, the data may include labels,
        which are the correct outputs paired with example data inputs.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.A.2</title>
      <p>
        Machine learning models use training data to learn patterns that connect inputs to outputs.
        These patterns come from the examples in the training data and, for supervised learning,
        the labels attached to them.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.A.3</title>
      <p>
        Training data is often split into separate sets so the model can learn from one set and
        then be evaluated on different data it has not seen before. The training set is used to
        build the model, the validation set is used to adjust model choices and check performance
        during development, and the test set is used for a final check of how well the model
        performs on new examples.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.A.4</title>
      <p>
        In supervised learning, training examples are paired with labels that represent the
        correct output. Using both examples that match the target and nonexamples that do not
        match the target helps the model learn boundaries and make more accurate distinctions.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.A.5</title>
      <p>
        Many machine learning models produce probabilistic outputs, such as a likelihood or
        confidence score, rather than a guaranteed answer. People decide how to use these scores
        by choosing a threshold, which determines when the system accepts a prediction automatically
        and when it requires more checking or human review.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.A.6</title>
      <p>
        When using a threshold, a system can make two kinds of mistakes. A false positive occurs
        when the system incorrectly accepts something that should be rejected. A false negative
        occurs when the system incorrectly rejects something that should be accepted. Changing
        the threshold usually reduces one kind of error while increasing the other, so developers
        choose thresholds by considering which error is more harmful for the user and the situation.
        Developers may also add human review or additional checks to reduce harm.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.A.7</title>
      <p>
        Training sets that include diverse examples and counterexamples help a model generalize,
        meaning it performs well on new inputs and varied real-world situations, not just the
        examples it learned from.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.A.8</title>
      <p>
        A model can appear to work well during development but still perform poorly on new inputs
        if it learns patterns that do not generalize beyond the training examples. Testing a model
        on new data helps evaluate whether its patterns transfer beyond the training sets.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="training-data-accuracy-privacy-bias">
    <title>Training Data, Accuracy, Privacy, and Bias</title>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.B.1</title>
      <p>
        Training data may be processed before a model is trained to decide which examples are
        included and how they are organized. Sampling can reduce data set size or balance the
        number of examples across categories. Sampling decisions can improve performance for
        underrepresented cases, but they can also reduce accuracy if important cases are removed
        or under-sampled.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.B.2</title>
      <p>
        Training data can expose sensitive information. Privacy protection includes minimizing,
        limiting access to, and protecting sensitive data during storage and sharing.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.B.3</title>
      <p>
        Bias can occur when training data does not represent the people or situations the model
        will be used for, or when labels reflect human assumptions. Collecting more data does not
        necessarily reduce bias if the same sampling methods or labeling choices continue.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>4.7.B.4</title>
      <p>
        Training data should reflect the intended users and real-world contexts where the model
        will be used. If the data set differs from actual use, the model can perform poorly or
        unevenly across users even if it appears accurate during testing. Evaluating training data
        includes checking whether it represents the people, environments, languages, and situations
        the system is meant to support, and testing with representative user scenarios to reduce
        confusion and unequal performance.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="LOs-4-7">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>

    <remark>
      <title>4.7.A</title>
      <p>
        Explain how data and probabilistic algorithms shape machine learning outputs.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>4.7.B</title>
      <p>
        Explain how training data decisions can affect accuracy, privacy, and bias.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

